// THE DRAIN BRACKET OVER ONE HOST, AS A PURE STEP MACHINE PLUS THE THREE EFFECTS IT SEQUENCES.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS (chain re-derivation, DESIGN 6b). The cap drop-ins are template-level, so write +
// daemon-reload re-applies the caps to every running slot and can OOM-kill a busy slot's live job. The
// route used to read idleness FIRST, with admission still open, and refuse on any busy slot. "Idle" read
// with admission open is a point-in-time fact that decays, and about half of a loaded host's slots are
// busy at any instant, so that refusal never cleared. The earliest unjustified boundary is the gate:
// idleness is only a stable predicate over an interval in which no new job is admitted. This module makes
// that interval an explicit, declared state: admission is closed FIRST, the live jobs are waited out
// inside a DECLARED bound, idleness is judged UNDER the closure, the write is made, and admission is
// re-opened on every exit path.
//
// ORDER: close admission (mask) -> wait, bounded, for every slot to be drained -> (the caller writes,
// reloads, activates, reads back from the cgroup files) -> re-admit (activate + read back).
//
// COULD-NOT-TELL IS NOT IDLE (DESIGN 5). An unreadable process listing or unit reading, and a slot that is
// not masked, are UNPROVEN: they hold the wait and, at the bound, refuse the host. They never authorize
// the write.
//
// THE ADMISSION CLOSURE IS A DECLARED FACT, NOT A HOPE. Masking stops successors only. An idle listener
// that is already running keeps polling for work, and stopping it races a job assignment whose loss
// cancels a real CI job on another PR's author. Two closures are declared:
//   AdmissionMaskOnly: a running idle listener is not stopped and nothing is written; the bracket refuses
//     AdmissionNotClosed naming the units. It stays a declared arm so the refusal it was has a control.
//   AdmissionMaskRosterAndNoWorker (gunbc.design_ruling runner_listener_admission_is_mask_roster_and_no_worker): a running idle listener is
//     ADMITTED to the write and to its stop/start only when THREE independent readings agree: its unit is
//     masked, the organization roster says its registration is busy=false, and its cgroup holds no
//     Runner.Worker. All three are re-read immediately before the stop. A reading that is unread or
//     disagrees refuses AdmissionNotClosed with its cause and never restarts. A busy listener (a live
//     Runner.Worker) is waited out as before. THERE IS NO DEREGISTER: nothing re-registers a long-lived
//     slot, so removing the registration would take the slot out of service for good.
//
// THE READ-TO-STOP WINDOW IS A TYPED RESIDUAL, NOT A CLOSURE (listener_restart_residual): GitHub can
// assign a job to the still-registered listener after the last reading and before the stop lands, and the
// stop then cancels that job. The bracket narrows that window to the readings' own latency and says so; it
// does not claim to close it. RE-REGISTRATION IS A DECLARED FRONTIER (listener_reregistration_frontier):
// the closing leg that would remove the residual needs an upstream atomic busy-refusing admission close.
//
// DIVERGES FROM std.temporal_effect HeldLease AND std.durable_exclusive_hold, WITH REASON. "This host is
// drained by this run" is a lease whose release is the re-admit readback (HostLeftState), but both
// carriers rest on a LeaseEpoch and a compare-and-set over a durable store; the host path has no durable
// store and its single writer is the dispatching workflow's concurrency group. A durable store for the
// hold is the trigger to inhabit them; no vocabulary is borrowed meanwhile, since a borrowed name with no
// epoch behind it would be a meaning fork.

type AdmissionClosure
  = AdmissionMaskOnly
  | AdmissionMaskRosterAndNoWorker

data runner_host_cap_admission_closure: AdmissionClosure = AdmissionMaskRosterAndNoWorker

// THE RESIDUAL IS THE WINDOW BETWEEN THE LAST READING AND THE STOP (rendered by the wire below), AND ITS
// FRONTIER IS TYPED: a fold can read which capabilities would restore the closure, and the wire renders
// FROM these rows. The closure is restored only when ALL listed capabilities have landed.
// the capabilities whose landing restores the closure; each is a consumed arm of AdmissionClosure when it lands
type ReregistrationTrigger
  = UpstreamAtomicBusyRefusingAdmissionClose
  | LongLivedSlotReregistrationRoute

// the readings after the last of which the window opens; the wire renders them, so the residual's population is data
// CurrentRegistrationReading is the fourth decay: #14877 joined the roster on the slot's CURRENT JIT identity
// (read from the host's .runner file), and that reading decays like the other three -- between its read and the
// stop the slot's registration identity can change (a re-registration replaces .runner), which makes the roster
// evidence pertain to an identity the host no longer carries. It is taken inside the same pass and re-read with
// it immediately before the stop, so it narrows, never closes -- exactly the residual's shape.
type ResidualReading
  = MaskReading
  | RosterBusyFalseReading
  | NoRunnerWorkerReading
  | CurrentRegistrationReading

type ListenerRestartResidual {
  window_opens_after: List<ResidualReading>
}

data listener_restart_residual: ListenerRestartResidual = ListenerRestartResidual { window_opens_after: [MaskReading, RosterBusyFalseReading, NoRunnerWorkerReading, CurrentRegistrationReading] }

fn residual_reading_wire(r: ResidualReading) -> String {
  match r { MaskReading => "mask" RosterBusyFalseReading => "roster busy=false" NoRunnerWorkerReading => "no Runner.Worker" CurrentRegistrationReading => "current registration" }
}

type ListenerReregistrationFrontier {
  triggers: List<ReregistrationTrigger>
}

data listener_reregistration_frontier: ListenerReregistrationFrontier = ListenerReregistrationFrontier { triggers: [UpstreamAtomicBusyRefusingAdmissionClose, LongLivedSlotReregistrationRoute] }

fn reregistration_trigger_wire(t: ReregistrationTrigger) -> String {
  match t {
    UpstreamAtomicBusyRefusingAdmissionClose => "an upstream-documented atomic busy-refusing admission close"
    LongLivedSlotReregistrationRoute => "a re-registration route for long-lived slots"
  }
}

fn listener_restart_residual_wire() -> String {
  let f = listener_reregistration_frontier
  join([
    "residual: a job GitHub assigns to a still-registered idle listener after the last of the readings (", join(map(listener_restart_residual.window_opens_after, residual_reading_wire), ", "), ") and before the stop lands is cancelled by the stop; the readings are re-read immediately before the stop to narrow that window, not to close it; ",
    "frontier: the closure is restored only when ", join(map(f.triggers, reregistration_trigger_wire), " AND "), " lands as a consumed arm of AdmissionClosure",
  ], "")
}

// THE WAIT BOUND IS DERIVED, NOT RATIFIED AS A NUMBER (operator ruling, 2026-10-23, relayed by
// sleek-owl-397). It must exceed the longest expected job on a runner slot, so a drain does not
// refuse on normal work — and the population of 'expected jobs' HAS an authority: the declared
// job-level timeouts of the fleet-targeted generated workflows. wait_bound is that fold — the
// maximum declared timeout over the jobs whose runner selects the fleet pool AND whose workflow
// carries an autonomous trigger (gunbc.runner.runner_fleet_job_timeout_census
// fleet_targeted_wait_bound), plus one poll interval (the wait is quantized to ticks; the last tick
// can sample up to a full interval early). It REPLACES the 2700s literal, whose row rested on the
// premise 'no source declares the longest job' — a premise falsified by
// gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow compiler_gate_emit_build_timeout_minutes (90), which is why
// srv3 cap converge run 39961292233 withheld every cap: a v2-bootstrap job runs ~55 minutes under a
// 90-minute declaration, and a 2700s drain bound refused it mid-flight. Today the fold yields
// 90 min (v2-bootstrap), so the bound is 90*60 + poll, and the cost of a cap change is that long of
// a host's slots out of admission — a rare event bounded by the fleet's own declared worst job.
//
// THE DISPATCH-ONLY RESIDUAL IS DECLARED, NOT IMPLIED (ruled by clever-crane-830, 2026-10-23): jobs
// whose workflows are workflow_dispatch-only — instrument-dispatch's census job
// (gunbc.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow instrument_dispatch_timeout_minutes) and
// fleet-converge's build job (gunbc.fleet.fleet_converge_workflow
// gunbc_fleet_converge_build_job_backstop_timeout_minutes) — are outside the population: GitHub
// cannot park them on a slot unprompted. If one is running when a drain starts it can exceed the
// bound (both are long census/release builds by design); the bracket then refuses TYPED (caps
// withheld, drain_bound_exceeded). That is fail-closed, and the operator who dispatches both owns
// the sequencing. The control test.claim.runner.runner_fleet_job_timeout_bound_witness goes red if
// any population member's declared timeout exceeds the bound, so a new autonomous fleet job cannot
// silently outlive the drain; the bound grows with the declaration by derivation.
type HostCapDrainPolicy {
  wait_bound: Second
  poll_interval: Second
  operations_allowance: Second
  release_allowance: Second
  cancellation_envelope: Second
  release_startup_allowance: Second
}

// THE OPERATIONS AND RELEASE ALLOWANCES ARE POLICY CHOICES TOO, each bounding what the wait bound does
// not: the write, daemon-reload, activation and cgroup readback after a drain (operations), and the
// re-admission with its readback (release). They are generous against a handful of ssh round trips per
// slot over 19 slots. The enclosing workflow step's timeout is a fold over exactly these three figures
// (host_cap_bracket_budget), so no second literal can disagree with the bracket.
// THE POLL INTERVAL IS THE POLICY'S, AND THE BOUND FOLDS OVER IT (see fleet_targeted_wait_bound):
// it is defined first so the derived wait bound reads the same field the drain loop ticks on, and no
// second literal can disagree with the bracket.
data runner_host_cap_drain_poll_interval: Second = second(count: 15)

data runner_host_cap_drain_policy: HostCapDrainPolicy = HostCapDrainPolicy {
  wait_bound: fleet_targeted_wait_bound(policy_poll_interval: runner_host_cap_drain_poll_interval),
  poll_interval: runner_host_cap_drain_poll_interval,
  operations_allowance: second(count: 600),
  release_allowance: second(count: 240),
  cancellation_envelope: cancelled_job_always_step_timeout,
  release_startup_allowance: second(count: gunbc_ci_gunbc_run_resolve_step_timeout_minutes * 60),
}

// THE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE THROUGH WHICH THE ENCLOSING STEP TELLS THE BRACKET ITS OWN DECLARED BUDGET, in
// seconds. The workflow derives the step timeout and this value from the same policy row.
data runner_host_cap_step_budget_env_name: String = "RUNNER_HOST_CAP_STEP_BUDGET_SECONDS"

// WHAT A STEP MUST HOLD FOR THE WHOLE BRACKET: drain, operations and release, in seconds.
fn host_cap_bracket_budget(policy: HostCapDrainPolicy) -> Second {
  second(count: second_count(s: policy.wait_bound) + second_count(s: policy.operations_allowance) + second_count(s: policy.release_allowance))
}

// REFUSE BEFORE MASKING when the budget the bracket was given cannot cover it: a step killed mid-wait
// leaves its masks, so a bracket that cannot finish must not begin.
type BracketBudgetVerdict
  = BudgetCovers
  | BudgetCannotCover { needed: Second, given: Second }

// THE RELEASE IS NOT GUARANTEED TO FIT THE CANCELLATION ENVELOPE, AND SAYS SO. GitHub gives a cancelled job's
// always() steps a 5-minute cancellation timeout and then force-cancels (docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/
// workflows-and-actions/expressions, status check functions; the figure is extdeps.github.actions cancelled_job_always_step_timeout). The release
// step is a gunbc run step: its startup is the workflow's declared resolve budget (one authority,
// gunbc_ci_gunbc_run_resolve_step_timeout_minutes), and the measured resolve of this closure is minutes, so
// the declared release does not fit. The standing is a typed residual, not a refusal and not a claim:
// refusing every bracket would make the cap unconvergeable, and claiming coverage would be false. The harm
// and the climb are the recurring_failure_mode row runner_host_cap_release_lost_to_cancellation.
type ReleaseEnvelopeStanding
  = ReleaseFitsEnvelope
  | ReleaseMayOutliveCancellation { needed: Second, envelope: Second }

fn release_envelope_standing(policy: HostCapDrainPolicy) -> ReleaseEnvelopeStanding {
  let needed = second_count(s: policy.release_allowance) + second_count(s: policy.release_startup_allowance)
  if needed <= second_count(s: policy.cancellation_envelope) { ReleaseFitsEnvelope } else { ReleaseMayOutliveCancellation { needed: second(count: needed), envelope: policy.cancellation_envelope } }
}

fn release_envelope_wire(policy: HostCapDrainPolicy) -> String {
  match release_envelope_standing(policy: policy) {
    ReleaseFitsEnvelope => "release fits the cancellation envelope"
    ReleaseMayOutliveCancellation { needed: n, envelope: e } => join(["residual: a cancelled job force-cancels the release after ", to_string(second_count(s: e)), "s and the release needs ", to_string(second_count(s: n)), "s, so a cancellation can leave the host masked until a human acts; restoration trigger: the next bracket run reading a durable hold the bracket took on the host slots (std.durable_exclusive_hold) and re-admitting the slots it names, with readback, before any new drain"], "")
  }
}

fn bracket_budget_verdict(policy: HostCapDrainPolicy, given: Second) -> BracketBudgetVerdict {
  let needed = host_cap_bracket_budget(policy: policy)
  if second_count(s: given) >= second_count(s: needed) { BudgetCovers } else { BudgetCannotCover { needed: needed, given: given } }
}

fn host_cap_drain_ticks(policy: HostCapDrainPolicy) -> Int {
  second_count(s: policy.wait_bound) / second_count(s: policy.poll_interval)
}

// ONE SLOT, AS READ AFTER ADMISSION WAS CLOSED: two independent facts, each a declared sum, so no
// combination the unit cannot be in is writable (a unit is not both stopping and a running listener).
// `admission` comes from the unit reading, `run` from the unit's state and its own process listing.
type SlotAdmissionState
  = AdmissionClosedByMask
  | AdmissionOpen

// WHAT THE ORGANIZATION ROSTER SAYS OF ONE LISTENER'S REGISTRATION, a reading of its own: a registration
// absent from the roster, a partial or unavailable roster, or two registrations of one name disagreeing on
// busy are never read as not-busy (could-not-tell is not idle).
// EVERY ROSTER READING NAMES THE REGISTRATION IT WAS JOINED ON. The join key is a JIT-minted suffixed
// identity, so a verdict that says only "the roster says busy" is not yet a located diagnostic (§5): an
// operator looking at a withheld cap cannot tell which of the slot's mints the bracket examined, and a
// slot re-mints per job. The name is carried forward from the one place it is read
// (listener_roster_reading_of, where the host's answer is in scope), not re-derived here -- it is the same
// value the join used, so the verdict cannot name an identity the join did not use.
//
// `joined` is a NonEmptyStr, not a String: it arrives from ListenerRegistrationObserved.name, which is
// already established non-empty by the decoder that produced it. Widening it to String here would discard
// a fact the source had already proven (§3b's preservation of facts from source through typing).
type ListenerRosterReading
  = RosterNotBusy { joined: NonEmptyStr }
  | RosterBusy { joined: NonEmptyStr }
  | RosterUnread { cause: String }
  | RosterDisagrees { cause: String }

type SlotRunState
  = RunStopped
  | RunStopping
  | RunListenerIdle { roster: ListenerRosterReading }
  | RunWorkerLive
  | RunUnobserved { cause: NonEmptyStr }

type SlotDrainReading {
  unit: NonEmptyStr
  admission: SlotAdmissionState
  run: SlotRunState
}

// THE ONE FOLD of a reading into what the bracket decides on. A running idle listener is the one the mask
// did not close; a stopping unit is unproven until it is stopped (its cgroup may still hold a listener);
// an unobserved run is never idle.
type SlotDrainClass
  = ClassDrained
  | ClassBusy
  | ClassListenerAdmitted { joined: NonEmptyStr }
  | ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: String }
  | ClassAdmissionOpen
  | ClassUnproven { cause: String }

fn listener_class(closure: AdmissionClosure, r: SlotDrainReading, roster: ListenerRosterReading) -> SlotDrainClass {
  match closure {
    AdmissionMaskOnly => ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: "mask-only closure: a running idle listener keeps polling for work" }
    AdmissionMaskRosterAndNoWorker =>
      match r.admission {
        AdmissionOpen => ClassAdmissionOpen
        AdmissionClosedByMask =>
          match roster {
            // BOTH verdicts name the identity. NotBusy is the one that ADMITS a cap write, so the
            // drained verdict has to say which registration it certified; Busy is the one that WITHHOLDS
            // it, so the refusal has to say which registration was found working.
            RosterNotBusy { joined: j } => ClassListenerAdmitted { joined: j }
            RosterBusy { joined: j } => ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: join(["the roster says the registration ", j as String, " is busy while its cgroup holds no Runner.Worker: the readings disagree"], "") }
            RosterUnread { cause: c } => ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: concat("the roster did not say whether the registration is busy: ", c) }
            RosterDisagrees { cause: c } => ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: concat("the roster readings disagree: ", c) }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn slot_drain_class(closure: AdmissionClosure, r: SlotDrainReading) -> SlotDrainClass {
  match r.run {
    RunUnobserved { cause: c } => ClassUnproven { cause: c as String }
    RunListenerIdle { roster: ro } => listener_class(closure: closure, r: r, roster: ro)
    RunWorkerLive => match r.admission { AdmissionClosedByMask => ClassBusy AdmissionOpen => ClassAdmissionOpen }
    RunStopping => match r.admission { AdmissionClosedByMask => ClassUnproven { cause: "the unit is stopping" } AdmissionOpen => ClassAdmissionOpen }
    RunStopped => match r.admission { AdmissionClosedByMask => ClassDrained AdmissionOpen => ClassAdmissionOpen }
  }
}

fn drain_class_is_idle_listener(c: SlotDrainClass) -> Bool { match c { ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: _ } => true _ => false } }
fn drain_class_is_drained(c: SlotDrainClass) -> Bool { match c { ClassDrained => true ClassListenerAdmitted { joined: _ } => true _ => false } }
fn drain_class_is_busy(c: SlotDrainClass) -> Bool { match c { ClassBusy => true _ => false } }
fn drain_class_is_unproven(c: SlotDrainClass) -> Bool { match c { ClassAdmissionOpen => true ClassUnproven { cause: _ } => true _ => false } }

type BracketWaitStep
  // `certified` IS the drained set: one typed record per admitted listener, pairing the unit with the
  // JIT-minted registration the roster was joined on. There is deliberately no separate `listeners` list --
  // the units are read out of these records, so the two cannot be computed by different routes and drift
  // apart while agreeing by luck (§3). Typed rather than a positional pairing, and rather than a
  // "unit=registration" String: the unit travels INSIDE the record, so no ordering can misattribute an
  // identity to the wrong unit, and neither part has to be recovered by parsing (§2: a String leaf hiding
  // named parts is anemic modeling). It travels with the verdict instead of being re-read, so the cap phase
  // and the receipt can say what was certified without asking the host again -- and in apply mode this is
  // the ONLY place that identity can reach an operator.
  = BracketDrained { certified: List<CertifiedListener> }
  | BracketWaiting { ticks_left: Int }
  | BracketAdmissionNotClosed { listeners: List<NonEmptyStr>, causes: List<String> }
  | BracketBoundExceeded { busy: List<NonEmptyStr>, unproven: List<NonEmptyStr> }

type ClassedReading {
  unit: NonEmptyStr
  class: SlotDrainClass
}

fn bracket_wait_step(closure: AdmissionClosure, readings: List<SlotDrainReading>, ticks_left: Int, time_up: Bool) -> BracketWaitStep {
  let classed = map(readings, r => ClassedReading { unit: r.unit, class: slot_drain_class(closure: closure, r: r) })
  let refused = filter(classed, c => drain_class_is_idle_listener(c: c.class))
  if length(refused) > 0 {
    BracketAdmissionNotClosed { listeners: map(refused, c => c.unit), causes: list_flat_map(classed, c => match c.class { ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: x } => [concat(c.unit as String, ": ", x)] _ => [] }) }
  } else if all(classed, c => drain_class_is_drained(c: c.class)) {
    // One record per admitted reading, from a single flat-map: the unit and the identity it was joined on
    // are bound at the source, so there is no positional pairing to drift and no default value fabricated
    // for a case the match excludes (a class that is not an admission contributes nothing, which is the
    // list monoid's identity rather than a made-up row).
    BracketDrained {
      certified: list_flat_map(classed, c => match c.class {
        ClassListenerAdmitted { joined: j } => [CertifiedListener { unit: c.unit, registration: j }]
        _ => []
      }),
    }
  } else if ticks_left <= 0 || time_up {
    BracketBoundExceeded {
      busy: map(filter(classed, c => drain_class_is_busy(c: c.class)), c => c.unit),
      unproven: map(filter(classed, c => drain_class_is_unproven(c: c.class)), c => c.unit),
    }
  } else {
    BracketWaiting { ticks_left: ticks_left - 1 }
  }
}

// WHAT THE HOST IS LEFT IN, stated on every result.
type HostLeftState
  = HostAdmitting
  | HostLeftDrained { units: List<NonEmptyStr> }

type HostCapBracketRefusal
  = BracketRefusedAdmissionNotClosed { listeners: List<NonEmptyStr>, causes: List<String> }
  | BracketRefusedBoundExceeded { busy: List<NonEmptyStr>, unproven: List<NonEmptyStr>, waited: Second }
  | BracketRefusedBudgetCannotCover { needed: Second, given: Second }
  | BracketRefusedClockUnreadable { detail: String }
  | BracketRefusedObligationNotRecorded { cause: String }

fn bracket_refusal_wire(r: HostCapBracketRefusal) -> String {
  match r {
    BracketRefusedAdmissionNotClosed { listeners: ls, causes: cs } => join(["admission is not closed: a running idle listener is admitted only when its mask, the roster's busy=false and an empty Runner.Worker listing agree, so no cap was written and nothing was restarted; listeners=", join(map(ls, u => u as String), " "), " causes=[", join(cs, "; "), "]; ", listener_restart_residual_wire()], "")
    BracketRefusedBoundExceeded { busy: b, unproven: u, waited: w } => join(["the drain bound of ", to_string(second_count(s: w)), "s was exceeded, so no cap was written; busy=", join(map(b, x => x as String), " "), " unproven=", join(map(u, x => x as String), " ")], "")
    BracketRefusedBudgetCannotCover { needed: n, given: g } => join(["the step budget of ", to_string(second_count(s: g)), "s cannot cover the bracket's ", to_string(second_count(s: n)), "s (drain + operations + release), so nothing was masked and no cap was written; the host is untouched"], "")
    BracketRefusedObligationNotRecorded { cause: c } => join(["the release obligation could not be recorded (", c, "), so nothing was masked and no cap was written; the host is untouched"], "")
    BracketRefusedClockUnreadable { detail: d } => join(["the wall clock could not be read (", d, "), so the total elapsed time cannot be bounded: nothing was masked and no cap was written; the host is untouched"], "")
  }
}

fn host_left_wire(l: HostLeftState) -> String {
  match l {
    HostAdmitting => "host left admitting (every slot unmasked, active and read back)"
    HostLeftDrained { units: us } => join(["host left DRAINED: still masked or not read back: ", join(map(us, u => u as String), " ")], "")
  }
}

fn readmit_unsettled(outcomes: List<SlotActuationOutcome>) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  map(filter(outcomes, o => !slot_actuation_settled(o: o)), o => match o {
    SlotActuationRefused { unit: u, step: _, reason: _ } => u
    SlotActuationNoop { unit: u, state: _ } => u
    SlotActivated { unit: u } => u
    SlotNotInstalled { unit: u } => u
    SlotDrainedAndMasked { unit: u } => u
    SlotDrainPending { unit: u, cause: _ } => u
  })
}

// WHAT THE HOST IS LEFT IN, from the re-admission readback alone: every unit needs exactly one settled
// outcome, so a refused, pending or missing one leaves the host DRAINED and names the units.
fn host_left_of_readmit(units: List<NonEmptyStr>, outcomes: List<SlotActuationOutcome>) -> HostLeftState {
  let stuck = readmit_unsettled(outcomes: outcomes)
  if length(stuck) == 0 && length(outcomes) == length(units) { HostAdmitting } else { HostLeftDrained { units: if length(stuck) == 0 { units } else { stuck } } }
}

// ── The effects ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A SLOT IS IDLE ONLY WHEN ITS UNIT IS STOPPED OR ITS CGROUP HOLDS NO RUNNER.WORKER. "Not running" is not
// "no worker": a deactivating unit is a stop in progress and its job process may still be alive, so every
// state but a stopped one demands the cgroup reading. A refused or unparsed show reading is unobserved,
// never idle.
fn slot_drain_needs_worker(read: SlotReadOutcome) -> Bool {
  match read {
    SlotReadRefused { reason: _ } => false
    SlotRead { reading: r } => !slot_state_stopped(active: r.active)
  }
}

// THE CURRENT REGISTRATION IDENTITY OF ONE SLOT'S LISTENER, read from the host before the roster is
// joined. This exists because a fleet listener does NOT register under the slot's name: it registers
// through a JIT mint (gunbc.runner.runner_jit_mint) whose name carries a per-job suffix, so the roster
// carries srv3-02-1792728034-201414 and never srv3-02. See gunbc.runner.runner_slot_actuation for the
// file that holds the answer and for why no prefix of the slot's name may stand in for it.
//
// NOT NEEDED IS ITS OWN CASE, not a placeholder for "unknown". The roster is read only for a slot that
// is a running idle listener, and so is this reading; a slot that never called for the roster has a
// reading that says exactly that, and if anything ever asked it for a verdict it would be refused rather
// than silently admitted on an identity nobody read.
// THE UNIT AND THE REGISTRATION IT REGISTERED UNDER, as one typed fact. Both parts are named and both
// keep the non-emptiness the host reading established; the receipt renders this, it does not parse it.
type CertifiedListener { unit: NonEmptyStr, registration: NonEmptyStr }

// THE ONE RENDERING of that pair, so no consumer invents its own separator or its own parsing.
fn certified_listener_line(c: CertifiedListener) -> String { join([c.unit as String, "=", c.registration as String], "") }

type ListenerRegistrationReading
  = ListenerRegistrationObserved { unit: NonEmptyStr, instance: NonEmptyStr, name: NonEmptyStr }
  | ListenerRegistrationUnreadable { unit: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }
  | ListenerRegistrationNotNeeded { unit: NonEmptyStr }

// THE ONE AUTHORITY FOR THE NAME THIS SLOT'S CURRENT LISTENER REGISTERED UNDER, asked of the host over
// the route every other slot reading uses. The slot is named through its UNIT: the unit is what the
// bracket and every refusal speak in, and the instance inside it is what addresses the registration
// file. An unparseable unit yields no identity to join on rather than a guess at one — and no file is
// read, because there is no slot whose tree to open.
fn slot_registration_reading_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> ListenerRegistrationReading
{
  match slot_current_registration_over_unit(target: target, context: context, unit: unit) {
    UnitRegistrationRead { unit: u, instance: i, name: n } => ListenerRegistrationObserved { unit: u, instance: i, name: n }
    UnitRegistrationUnreadable { unit: u, cause: c } => ListenerRegistrationUnreadable { unit: u, cause: c }
  }
}

// THE JOIN, AND ONLY THE JOIN MAY SAY IDLE. The roster is joined on the CURRENT registration identity by
// EXACT equality — never on a prefix, and never on the slot's bare name. A prefix is a second, weaker
// key: every mint this slot has ever run shares it, so a prefix join can declare "idle" about a
// registration this host already replaced while the registration that is actually answering jobs goes
// unexamined, and the bracket would then write a drop-in and restart the unit out from under a job
// another PR's author is waiting on. Exact identity is what makes "not on the roster" mean something:
// it can then only mean that THIS name, this mint, is not there.
//
// NO READABLE IDENTITY IS A REFUSAL THAT NAMES THE SLOT, never a fallback to the bare name. The three
// roster-level causes (not read, partial, reader unavailable) keep priority: they say the roster itself
// is unseen, which is a different failure than the roster having been seen and this slot's registration
// missing from it. Each carries the slot and, where the host yielded one, the suffixed identity — so a
// withheld cap can be diagnosed from the log line alone, and "between jobs" is distinguishable from
// "the roster was never read".
fn listener_roster_reading_of(reading: ListenerRegistrationReading, evidence: OrganizationRunnerRosterEvidence?) -> ListenerRosterReading {
  match evidence {
    Absent => RosterUnread { cause: "the roster was not read" }
    Present { value: RosterReadUnavailable { reason: why } } => RosterUnread { cause: why }
    Present { value: RosterObservedPartial { observed_on: _, registrations: _, observed_count: o, authority_total: t } } =>
      RosterUnread { cause: join(["the roster was read partially (", to_string(o), " of ", to_string(t), "), so an unread registration cannot be excluded"], "") }
    Present { value: RosterObserved { observed_on: _, registrations: regs } } =>
      match reading {
        ListenerRegistrationNotNeeded { unit: u } =>
          RosterUnread { cause: join([u as String, ": no current registration was read for this slot, so nothing may be joined on its behalf"], "") }
        ListenerRegistrationUnreadable { unit: u, cause: c } => RosterUnread { cause: concat(u as String, ": ", c) }
        ListenerRegistrationObserved { unit: u, instance: i, name: n } => {
          let hits = filter(regs, g => (g.name as String) == (n as String))
          if length(hits) == 0 {
            RosterUnread { cause: join([u as String, ": the registration ", n as String, " that ", i as String, " is running under now is ABSENT from the roster, which is not an idle slot but a registration between mints, and the roster cannot exclude a re-mint landing a job on it"], "") }
          }
          // The two admitting readings keep the TYPED identity, not the string rendering of it.
          else if all(hits, g => !g.busy) { RosterNotBusy { joined: n } }
          else if all(hits, g => g.busy) { RosterBusy { joined: n } }
          else { RosterDisagrees { cause: concat("registrations of the name ", n as String, " disagree on busy") } }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn slot_drain_reading_of(unit: NonEmptyStr, read: SlotReadOutcome, worker: SlotActivityObservation, roster: ListenerRosterReading) -> SlotDrainReading {
  match read {
    SlotReadRefused { reason: why } => SlotDrainReading { unit: unit, admission: AdmissionOpen, run: RunUnobserved { cause: why as NonEmptyStr } }
    SlotRead { reading: r } =>
      SlotDrainReading {
        unit: unit,
        admission: if slot_is_masked(r: r) { AdmissionClosedByMask } else { AdmissionOpen },
        run: slot_run_state_of(stopped: slot_state_stopped(active: r.active), stopping: slot_state_stopping(active: r.active), worker: worker, roster: roster),
      }
  }
}

fn slot_run_state_of(stopped: Bool, stopping: Bool, worker: SlotActivityObservation, roster: ListenerRosterReading) -> SlotRunState {
  if stopped { RunStopped } else {
    match worker {
      SlotHasLiveWorker => RunWorkerLive
      SlotActivityUnobserved { cause: c } => RunUnobserved { cause: c }
      SlotIdle => if stopping { RunStopping } else { RunListenerIdle { roster: roster } }
    }
  }
}

// ONE SLOT AS THE UNIT AND ITS PROCESS LISTING SHOW IT, before the roster is consulted: the roster is read
// only when some slot is a running idle listener, because it is the one reading that costs an API call.
type SlotShape {
  unit: NonEmptyStr
  read: SlotReadOutcome
  worker: SlotActivityObservation
}

fn read_slot_shape_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> SlotShape
{
  let read = read_slot_over(target: target, context: context, unit: unit)
  SlotShape {
    unit: unit,
    read: read,
    worker: if slot_drain_needs_worker(read: read) { read_slot_worker_over(target: target, context: context, unit: unit) } else { SlotIdle },
  }
}

fn slot_shape_is_idle_listener(sh: SlotShape) -> Bool {
  match slot_drain_reading_of(unit: sh.unit, read: sh.read, worker: sh.worker, roster: RosterUnread { cause: "shape" }).run {
    RunListenerIdle { roster: _ } => true
    _ => false
  }
}

// EVERY READING OF A PASS, THE ROSTER AND THE CURRENT REGISTRATIONS INCLUDED: the unit reads first, then
// (only if a slot is a running idle listener) that slot's current registration identity from the host, then
// — once, if any slot asked for it — one organization roster read through the production reader, then the
// fold. The mask, the registrations and the process listing are therefore read within one pass, back to
// back, which is what makes the join mean something: a registration read an hour before the roster would
// describe a mint the host may have replaced. A slot that is not an idle listener gets a reading of
// NotNeeded and costs no leg, exactly as it costs no roster call.
type SlotPass {
  shape: SlotShape
  registration: ListenerRegistrationReading
}

fn slot_pass_of(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, sh: SlotShape) -> SlotPass {
  SlotPass {
    shape: sh,
    registration: if slot_shape_is_idle_listener(sh: sh) { slot_registration_reading_over(target: target, context: context, unit: sh.unit) } else { ListenerRegistrationNotNeeded { unit: sh.unit } },
  }
}

fn read_slot_drain_pass_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, units: List<NonEmptyStr>) -> List<SlotDrainReading>
{
  let shapes = map(units, u => read_slot_shape_over(target: target, context: context, unit: u))
  let pass = map(shapes, sh => slot_pass_of(target: target, context: context, sh: sh))
  let needs_roster = any(shapes, sh => slot_shape_is_idle_listener(sh: sh))
  let evidence: OrganizationRunnerRosterEvidence? = if needs_roster { Present { value: organization_runner_roster_live_read() } } else { none }
  map(pass, p => slot_drain_reading_of(unit: p.shape.unit, read: p.shape.read, worker: p.shape.worker, roster: listener_roster_reading_of(reading: p.registration, evidence: evidence)))
}

// THE OWNERSHIP READ needs mask and liveness only, so it consults no roster.
fn read_slot_drain_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> SlotDrainReading
{
  let sh = read_slot_shape_over(target: target, context: context, unit: unit)
  slot_drain_reading_of(unit: unit, read: sh.read, worker: sh.worker, roster: RosterUnread { cause: "the roster is not consulted for ownership" })
}

// MASK ONLY, NEVER STOP: the mask inhibits successors and is safe at any time. Stopping a running idle
// listener races a job assignment, so this route never takes it (DrainSteps in the shared drain actuator
// would, for a slot whose job finished mid-wait). A failed mask is not reported here: the next reading
// shows the slot unmasked, and an unmasked slot is never drained.
fn mask_slot_only_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> String?
{
  match read_slot_over(target: target, context: context, unit: unit) {
    SlotReadRefused { reason: why } => Present { value: why }
    SlotRead { reading: r } => if slot_is_masked(r: r) { none } else { run_slot_steps_over(target: target, context: context, steps: [systemctl_mask_argv(unit: unit)]) }
  }
}

// TWO POPULATIONS, NEVER ONE: `observed` is every desired unit, because a cap written under a unit that
// still runs a Runner.Worker (or a running idle listener) is the hazard whoever masked it, so every one
// is read and judged; `owned` is the units THIS attempt masks, and only those are ever masked or released,
// so a mask someone else holds is neither taken over nor lifted.
// CLOSE ADMISSION, THEN WAIT. The first pass only READS: under a mask-only closure a running idle
// listener refuses the host before anything is changed, because stopping it races a job assignment.
// Later passes only MASK every slot; nothing is ever stopped, so a slot leaves service only when its own
// job ends and the mask blocks its restart. Each pass is judged by bracket_wait_step.
fn drain_until_quiescent_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, observed: List<NonEmptyStr>, owned: List<NonEmptyStr>, policy: HostCapDrainPolicy, ticks_left: Int, started: EpochMs) -> BracketWaitStep
{
  let closure = runner_host_cap_admission_closure
  let before = read_slot_drain_pass_over(target: target, context: context, units: observed)
  match bracket_wait_step(closure: closure, readings: before, ticks_left: ticks_left + 1, time_up: false) {
    BracketAdmissionNotClosed { listeners: ls, causes: cs } => BracketAdmissionNotClosed { listeners: ls, causes: cs }
    BracketDrained { certified: cs } => BracketDrained { certified: cs }
    BracketWaiting { ticks_left: _ } => drain_pass_over(target: target, context: context, observed: observed, owned: owned, policy: policy, ticks_left: ticks_left, started: started)
    BracketBoundExceeded { busy: b, unproven: u } => drain_pass_over(target: target, context: context, observed: observed, owned: owned, policy: policy, ticks_left: ticks_left, started: started)
  }
}

// THE TOTAL ELAPSED TIME IS BOUNDED, NOT ONLY THE POLL COUNT: reads and masks take time of their own, so
// a count of 15s sleeps undercounts. The deadline is the wait bound from the instant the bracket began; an
// unreadable clock counts as past it (could-not-tell is not still-inside-the-bound).
fn drain_time_up_over(policy: HostCapDrainPolicy, started: EpochMs) -> Bool
{
  match observer_clock_read() {
    ObserverClockUnreadable { detail: _ } => true
    ObserverClockObserved { instant: i } => ((observer_instant_millis(instant: i) as Int) - (started as Int)) >= second_count(s: policy.wait_bound) * 1000
  }
}

fn drain_pass_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, observed: List<NonEmptyStr>, owned: List<NonEmptyStr>, policy: HostCapDrainPolicy, ticks_left: Int, started: EpochMs) -> BracketWaitStep
{
  let _masked = map(owned, u => mask_slot_only_over(target: target, context: context, unit: u))
  let readings = read_slot_drain_pass_over(target: target, context: context, units: observed)
  match bracket_wait_step(closure: runner_host_cap_admission_closure, readings: readings, ticks_left: ticks_left, time_up: drain_time_up_over(policy: policy, started: started)) {
    BracketWaiting { ticks_left: n } => {
      let _paced = sleep_delay_seconds_second_carrier_projection(duration: policy.poll_interval)
      drain_pass_over(target: target, context: context, observed: observed, owned: owned, policy: policy, ticks_left: n, started: started)
    }
    step => step
  }
}

// THE START OF THE BRACKET, or the refusal that stops it before anything is masked.
type BracketStart
  = BracketStartAt { started: EpochMs }
  | BracketStartRefused { refusal: HostCapBracketRefusal }

fn bracket_start_over(policy: HostCapDrainPolicy, given: Second) -> BracketStart
{
  match bracket_budget_verdict(policy: policy, given: given) {
    BudgetCannotCover { needed: n, given: g } => BracketStartRefused { refusal: BracketRefusedBudgetCannotCover { needed: n, given: g } }
    BudgetCovers =>
      match observer_clock_read() {
        ObserverClockUnreadable { detail: d } => BracketStartRefused { refusal: BracketRefusedClockUnreadable { detail: d } }
        ObserverClockObserved { instant: i } => BracketStartAt { started: observer_instant_millis(instant: i) }
      }
  }
}

// ── The listener restart ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE STOP OF AN ADMITTED IDLE LISTENER, GATED BY A FRESH READING OF ALL THREE FACTS. The drain judged the
// listener admitted before the write; this reads the mask, the roster and the process listing again
// immediately before the stop, because the earlier reading decays. Only a listener still admitted is
// stopped (the activation that follows starts it, which is what applies the caps). A listener that is no
// longer admitted, or whose reading is unread or disagreeing, is NOT stopped: the refusal names the cause
// and the residual. A listener that is no longer running needs no stop.
type ListenerRestart
  = ListenerStopped { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | ListenerNoLongerRunning { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | ListenerStopRefused { unit: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

fn listener_stop_cause(c: SlotDrainClass) -> String {
  match c {
    ClassIdleListenerOpen { cause: x } => x
    ClassBusy => "a Runner.Worker is live in its cgroup"
    ClassAdmissionOpen => "the unit is no longer masked"
    ClassUnproven { cause: x } => x
    ClassDrained => "drained"
    ClassListenerAdmitted { joined: _ } => "admitted"
  }
}

// What the re-read before the stop DECIDES, kept apart from what the stop DID (ListenerRestart): nothing has been
// stopped when a stop is only admitted.
type ListenerStopDecision
  = ListenerStopAdmitted
  | ListenerAlreadyNotRunning
  | ListenerStopDeclined { cause: String }

fn restart_decision_of(closure: AdmissionClosure, unit: NonEmptyStr, readings: List<SlotDrainReading>) -> ListenerStopDecision {
  match first(filter(readings, r => (r.unit as String) == (unit as String))) {
    Absent => ListenerStopDeclined { cause: "the unit was not read immediately before the stop" }
    Present { value: r } =>
      match slot_drain_class(closure: closure, r: r) {
        ClassListenerAdmitted { joined: _ } => ListenerStopAdmitted
        ClassDrained => ListenerAlreadyNotRunning
        c => ListenerStopDeclined { cause: listener_stop_cause(c: c) }
      }
  }
}

fn stop_admitted_listener_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> ListenerRestart
{
  match restart_decision_of(closure: runner_host_cap_admission_closure, unit: unit, readings: read_slot_drain_pass_over(target: target, context: context, units: [unit])) {
    ListenerStopAdmitted =>
      match run_slot_steps_over(target: target, context: context, steps: [systemctl_stop_argv(unit: unit)]) {
        Present { value: why } => ListenerStopRefused { unit: unit, cause: concat("the stop failed: ", why) }
        Absent => ListenerStopped { unit: unit }
      }
    ListenerAlreadyNotRunning => ListenerNoLongerRunning { unit: unit }
    ListenerStopDeclined { cause: c } => ListenerStopRefused { unit: unit, cause: c }
  }
}

fn listener_restart_unit(r: ListenerRestart) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match r { ListenerStopped { unit: u } => u ListenerNoLongerRunning { unit: u } => u ListenerStopRefused { unit: u, cause: _ } => u }
}

fn listener_restart_refusal(r: ListenerRestart) -> String? {
  match r {
    ListenerStopRefused { unit: _, cause: c } => Present { value: join(["the listener was not restarted: ", c, "; ", listener_restart_residual_wire()], "") }
    _ => none
  }
}

// THE GATE ON THE FIRST CAP-APPLYING EFFECT. A cap write and the manager reload after it apply a cap to a
// RUNNING unit, so the ruling-B reading is taken again for every observed unit immediately before the write.
// An owned unit must still read as an admitted idle listener or as drained. A unit someone else masked is
// not ours to stop or start, so a foreign unit that is a live listener is refused: the reload would give it a cap
// this run can neither restart it under nor prove. A foreign unit that is not running is not refused.
type CapWriteGate
  = CapWriteAdmitted
  | CapWriteRefused { causes: List<String> }

fn cap_write_unit_cause(closure: AdmissionClosure, owned: List<NonEmptyStr>, unit: NonEmptyStr, readings: List<SlotDrainReading>) -> String? {
  let is_owned = any(owned, o => (o as String) == (unit as String))
  match first(filter(readings, r => (r.unit as String) == (unit as String))) {
    Absent => Present { value: join([unit as String, " was not read immediately before the first cap-applying effect"], "") }
    Present { value: r } =>
      match slot_drain_class(closure: closure, r: r) {
        ClassDrained => Absent
        ClassListenerAdmitted { joined: _ } =>
          if is_owned { Absent } else { Present { value: join([unit as String, " is masked by someone else and is a live listener: it is not stopped or restarted here, so a reload would cap it unproven"], "") } }
        c => Present { value: join([unit as String, ": ", listener_stop_cause(c: c)], "") }
      }
  }
}

fn cap_write_gate_of(closure: AdmissionClosure, observed: List<NonEmptyStr>, owned: List<NonEmptyStr>, readings: List<SlotDrainReading>) -> CapWriteGate {
  let causes = list_flat_map(observed, u => match cap_write_unit_cause(closure: closure, owned: owned, unit: u, readings: readings) { Present { value: c } => [c] Absent => [] })
  if length(causes) == 0 { CapWriteAdmitted } else { CapWriteRefused { causes: causes } }
}

// ── The release obligation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// WHAT THIS ATTEMPT OWES THE HOST, ESTABLISHED AFTER ADMISSION AND BEFORE THE FIRST MASK. The owned set is
// the units read as unmasked at that instant, so a unit this attempt never masked, a unit someone else
// masked, and a fabric member are all outside it by construction. The obligation is a file under the
// runner's temp dir keyed to the run, the attempt and the host: a step output would be published only at
// step end and so lost to a step killed mid-mask. A stale file (another run or attempt) is never trusted,
// and a refusal before acquisition leaves no file, so the release then starts no activation and no reload.
type BracketObligation {
  key: String
  units: List<NonEmptyStr>
}

data runner_host_cap_obligation_file_name: String = "runner-host-cap-release-obligation"
data runner_host_cap_runner_temp_env_name: String = "RUNNER_TEMP"

fn bracket_obligation_key(run_id: String, attempt: String, host: String) -> String {
  join(["run=", run_id, " attempt=", attempt, " host=", host], "")
}

// ONE CODEC: the obligation file is a JSON object with exactly the members key, discharged and units. The
// writer and the reader are the two directions of this one declaration (extdeps.languages.json), and the
// reader refuses a document that is not exactly this shape.
fn bracket_obligation_text(o: BracketObligation, discharged: Bool) -> String {
  serialize_json(v: json_object(members: [json_kv(key: "key", value: json_string(s: o.key)), json_kv(key: "discharged", value: json_bool(b: discharged)), json_kv(key: "units", value: json_array(elements: map(o.units, u => json_string(s: u as String))))]))
}

type ObligationReading
  = ObligationAbsent
  | ObligationHeld { units: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | ObligationDischarged
  | ObligationStale { found: String }
  | ObligationUnreadable { cause: String }

fn obligation_reading_of_text(text: String, expected_key: String) -> ObligationReading {
  match parse_json_document(s: text) {
    JsonDocumentUnreadable { gap: g } => ObligationUnreadable { cause: concat("the obligation file is not a JSON document: ", json_document_gap_text(gap: g)) }
    JsonDocumentParsed { value: doc } =>
      match doc {
        JsonObject { members: ms } =>
          if length(ms) != 3 {
            ObligationUnreadable { cause: "the obligation file does not carry exactly the members key, discharged and units" }
          } else {
            match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: "key") {
              JsonMemberFound { value: JsonString { value: k } } =>
                match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: "discharged") {
                  JsonMemberFound { value: JsonBool { value: d } } =>
                    match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: "units") {
                      JsonMemberFound { value: JsonArray { elements: es } } => obligation_reading_of_members(key: k, discharged: d, elements: es, expected_key: expected_key)
                      _ => ObligationUnreadable { cause: "the obligation file's units member is missing, duplicated or not an array" }
                    }
                  _ => ObligationUnreadable { cause: "the obligation file's discharged member is missing, duplicated or not a boolean" }
                }
              _ => ObligationUnreadable { cause: "the obligation file's key member is missing, duplicated or not a string" }
            }
          }
        _ => ObligationUnreadable { cause: "the obligation file is not a JSON object" }
      }
  }
}

fn obligation_reading_of_members(key: String, discharged: Bool, elements: List<JsonValue>, expected_key: String) -> ObligationReading {
  let names = map(elements, e => match e { JsonString { value: n } => n _ => "" })
  if key != expected_key {
    ObligationStale { found: key }
  } else if length(filter(names, n => n == "")) > 0 {
    ObligationUnreadable { cause: "the obligation file lists a unit that is not a non-empty string" }
  } else if discharged {
    ObligationDischarged
  } else if length(names) == 0 {
    ObligationUnreadable { cause: "the held obligation file lists no unit" }
  } else {
    ObligationHeld { units: map(names, n => n as NonEmptyStr) }
  }
}

fn obligation_reading_of(observation: FilesystemFileObservation, expected_key: String) -> ObligationReading {
  match observation {
    FilesystemFileAbsent(_) => ObligationAbsent
    FilesystemFileRead { path: _, content: c } => obligation_reading_of_text(text: c, expected_key: expected_key)
    FilesystemFileIndeterminate { cause: c } => ObligationUnreadable { cause: c }
    FilesystemFileObservationsDisagree { path: _, cause: c } => ObligationUnreadable { cause: c }
    FilesystemFileSubjectRefused { directory: _, name: _, cause: c } => ObligationUnreadable { cause: c }
  }
}

// THE RELEASE DECISION. Only a held obligation owes anything; absent owes nothing and starts nothing; a
// stale or unreadable file is a failure that names itself (could-not-tell is not "nothing owed").
type ReleaseDecision
  = ReleaseNothingOwed
  | ReleaseOwes { units: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | ReleaseAlreadyDischarged
  | ReleaseObligationUntrusted { cause: String }

fn release_decision_of(reading: ObligationReading) -> ReleaseDecision {
  match reading {
    ObligationAbsent => ReleaseNothingOwed
    ObligationHeld { units: us } => ReleaseOwes { units: us }
    ObligationDischarged => ReleaseAlreadyDischarged
    ObligationStale { found: f } => ReleaseObligationUntrusted { cause: concat("the obligation file belongs to another run or attempt and is not trusted: ", f) }
    ObligationUnreadable { cause: c } => ReleaseObligationUntrusted { cause: concat("the obligation file could not be read: ", c) }
  }
}

// WHICH UNITS ARE OWNED: unmasked and read. A unit whose reading is refused is unproven and refuses the
// acquisition (could-not-tell is not owned-by-nobody); a unit already masked belongs to someone else.
type OwnedSetVerdict
  = OwnedSet { units: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | OwnedSetUnproven { units: List<NonEmptyStr> }

fn slot_admission_is_open(r: SlotDrainReading) -> Bool { match r.admission { AdmissionOpen => true AdmissionClosedByMask => false } }
fn slot_run_is_unobserved(r: SlotDrainReading) -> Bool { match r.run { RunUnobserved { cause: _ } => true _ => false } }

fn owned_set_of(readings: List<SlotDrainReading>) -> OwnedSetVerdict {
  let unread = filter(readings, r => slot_admission_is_open(r: r) && slot_run_is_unobserved(r: r))
  if length(unread) > 0 { OwnedSetUnproven { units: map(unread, r => r.unit) } } else {
    OwnedSet { units: map(filter(readings, r => slot_admission_is_open(r: r)), r => r.unit) }
  }
}

// THE ONE RELEASE POLICY, used by every exit path (the finalizer, a refusal, a failed write): a unit this
// attempt masked is activated (unmask, enable, start) and read back; a unit that is NOT masked gets no argv,
// but its admission is still JUDGED from the reading, because "not masked" is not "admitting": a unit that
// was unmasked and then failed to start, or is disabled, or is not loaded, is a named refusal that leaves
// the host drained. The judgment is the activation readback authority (slot_activation_read_back_holds),
// not a second definition of admitting. An unreadable show is not "not masked": it falls to activation,
// which reads again and refuses with the read cause.
fn release_slot_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> SlotActuationOutcome
{
  match read_slot_over(target: target, context: context, unit: unit) {
    SlotReadRefused { reason: why } => SlotActuationRefused { unit: unit, step: "release-read", reason: concat("the release read of the unit was refused, so no release was decided and no argv was issued: ", why) }
    SlotRead { reading: r } =>
      if slot_is_masked(r: r) { activate_runner_slot_over(target: target, context: context, unit: unit) }
      else if slot_activation_read_back_holds(r: r) {
        SlotActuationNoop { unit: unit, state: "not masked and read back loaded, enabled and active: no argv issued" }
      } else {
        SlotActuationRefused { unit: unit, step: "readback", reason: "not masked, but not loaded, enabled and active: the unit is out of service and no argv was issued" }
      }
  }
}

fn release_owned_slots_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, units: List<NonEmptyStr>) -> List<SlotActuationOutcome>
{
  map(units, u => release_slot_over(target: target, context: context, unit: u))
}

// THE RELEASE RESULT, PURE: what the finalizer exits with and what its receipt says, from the decision and
// the per-unit outcomes alone. Only a held obligation issues anything; a stale or unreadable one fails.
type ReleaseVerdict
  = ReleaseVerdictSettled { summary: String }
  | ReleaseVerdictFailed { summary: String }

fn release_verdict_of(decision: ReleaseDecision, outcomes: List<SlotActuationOutcome>) -> ReleaseVerdict {
  match decision {
    ReleaseNothingOwed => ReleaseVerdictSettled { summary: "nothing owed: no obligation file, no argv issued" }
    ReleaseAlreadyDischarged => ReleaseVerdictSettled { summary: "obligation already discharged after an admitting readback, no argv issued" }
    ReleaseObligationUntrusted { cause: c } => ReleaseVerdictFailed { summary: c }
    ReleaseOwes { units: us } =>
      match host_left_of_readmit(units: us, outcomes: outcomes) {
        HostAdmitting => ReleaseVerdictSettled { summary: host_left_wire(l: HostAdmitting) }
        left => ReleaseVerdictFailed { summary: host_left_wire(l: left) }
      }
  }
}

fn release_verdict_is_settled(v: ReleaseVerdict) -> Bool { match v { ReleaseVerdictSettled { summary: _ } => true ReleaseVerdictFailed { summary: _ } => false } }

fn release_verdict_summary(v: ReleaseVerdict) -> String { match v { ReleaseVerdictSettled { summary: s } => s ReleaseVerdictFailed { summary: s } => s } }

fn release_decision_wire(d: ReleaseDecision) -> String {
  match d {
    ReleaseNothingOwed => "nothing-owed"
    ReleaseAlreadyDischarged => "already-discharged"
    ReleaseObligationUntrusted { cause: _ } => "obligation-untrusted"
    ReleaseOwes { units: us } => join(["owes ", join(map(us, u => u as String), " ")], "")
  }
}

// THE RELEASE RECEIPT TEXT: the attempt it belongs to, the host, the decision, each unit's outcome, the
// verdict, and the residual the bracket accepted (cancellation may outlive the release).
fn release_receipt_text(attempt: String, host: String, decision: ReleaseDecision, outcomes: List<SlotActuationOutcome>, verdict: ReleaseVerdict) -> String {
  join([
    "runner_host_cap_release attempt=", attempt, " host=", host, "\n",
    "decision=", release_decision_wire(d: decision), "\n",
    join(map(outcomes, o => concat(slot_actuation_outcome_wire(o: o), "\n")), ""),
    "verdict=", if release_verdict_is_settled(v: verdict) { "settled" } else { "FAILED" }, " ", release_verdict_summary(v: verdict), "\n",
    release_envelope_wire(policy: runner_host_cap_drain_policy), "\n",
  ], "")
}

// ── Wet: the obligation file ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn runner_host_cap_obligation_identity(host: String) -> String?
{
  match actions_variable_read(name: "GITHUB_RUN_ID") {
    ActionsVariableAbsent { variable: _, detail: _ } => none
    ActionsVariablePresent { value: run } =>
      match actions_variable_read(name: "GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT") {
        ActionsVariableAbsent { variable: _, detail: _ } => none
        ActionsVariablePresent { value: attempt } => Present { value: bracket_obligation_key(run_id: run, attempt: attempt, host: host) }
      }
  }
}

fn runner_host_cap_obligation_path() -> String?
{
  match actions_variable_read(name: runner_host_cap_runner_temp_env_name) {
    ActionsVariableAbsent { variable: _, detail: _ } => none
    ActionsVariablePresent { value: dir } => Present { value: join([dir, "/", runner_host_cap_obligation_file_name], "") }
  }
}

// AN EMPTY OWNED SET OWES NOTHING AND WRITES NOTHING: the decoder rejects an empty held list, so a writer
// that emitted one would refuse its own inverse. Absent then means nothing owed, which is true.
fn write_bracket_obligation(host: String, units: List<NonEmptyStr>, discharged: Bool) -> String?
{
  if length(units) == 0 { none } else {
  match runner_host_cap_obligation_identity(host: host) {
    Absent => Present { value: "GITHUB_RUN_ID or GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT is unset" }
    Present { value: key } =>
      match runner_host_cap_obligation_path() {
        Absent => Present { value: "RUNNER_TEMP is unset" }
        Present { value: path } => {
          let w = Filesystem.Write(path: path, content: bracket_obligation_text(o: BracketObligation { key: key, units: units }, discharged: discharged))
          if w.success { none } else { Present { value: w.error } }
        }
      }
  }
  }
}

fn read_bracket_obligation(host: String) -> ObligationReading
{
  match runner_host_cap_obligation_identity(host: host) {
    Absent => ObligationUnreadable { cause: "GITHUB_RUN_ID or GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT is unset" }
    Present { value: key } =>
      match runner_host_cap_obligation_path() {
        Absent => ObligationUnreadable { cause: "RUNNER_TEMP is unset" }
        Present { value: path } => obligation_reading_of(observation: filesystem_file_observation_of_path(path: path), expected_key: key)
      }
  }
}

// ACQUIRE: read every slot, judge the owned set, and record the obligation BEFORE the first mask. A
// refusal here has masked nothing and written nothing.
type BracketAcquisition
  = BracketAcquired { owned: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | BracketNotAcquired { refusal: HostCapBracketRefusal }

// WHICH SLOT DOES THE BRACKET ITSELF RUN ON? The fleet converge job is host-pinned: it executes inside a
// slot OF THE HOST IT CONVERGES (fleet_ssh_locus: the subject host's slot attests to its own state). The
// bracket therefore cannot drain, stop or restart one of its own units -- a slot is permanently busy with
// its own Runner.Worker, and stopping it would kill this very run. Before #holder-exclusion the bracket
// tried anyway: run 40085967610 drained its own slot srv3-13 until the 5415s bound, withheld caps for the
// WHOLE host, and every srv3/srv4 PR that landed there reds at the memory envelope as a result. Excluding
// the holder is safe only if the exclusion names the RIGHT unit, so identification joins three
// independent facts and fails closed, LOUDLY, if any is missing, unread or disagreeing:
//   1. the cgroup leaf this process lives in (Filesystem.Read of /proc/self/cgroup -- the sanctioned
//      production idiom, gunbc.dispatch_credential_acquisition's producer identity), parsed by the ONE
//      runner-unit parser so a non-runner cgroup can never name a runner unit;
//   2. the runner name this Actions job carries in its own environment (RUNNER_NAME); and
//   3. what that unit's CURRENT registration file says on the host (the same reading the drain certifies
//      listeners with), so a stale environment cannot point the exclusion at somebody else's slot.
// A slot added after the last converge, or one whose registration moved under the job, fails leg 3 and
// withholds: an unverifiable holder is an unverifiable exclusion, and a wrong exclusion lets the bracket
// unmask or restart a slot it never drained -- the accident class this whole module is built to refuse.
type BracketHolder
  = HolderIdentified { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | HolderUnidentified { cause: String }

// THE LEAF-TO-UNIT STEP, PURE. The cgroup leaf of a runner job is the unit name itself
// (systemd.slice/actions-runner@<instance>.service); the ONE parser in gunbc.runner.runner_unit decides
// whether the leaf is a runner unit at all, and the unit string is rebuilt from the parser's own
// instance, so what comes out is exactly the name the bracket speaks in its argv and refusals.
fn bracket_holder_leaf(relative_path: String) -> String {
  fold(split(s: relative_path, delimiter: "/"), init: "", f: (acc, part) => if trim(s: part) == "" { acc } else { trim(s: part) })
}

fn bracket_holder_unit_of_leaf(leaf: String) -> BracketHolder
{
  match read_runner_unit_name(observed: leaf as NonEmptyStr) {
    NotARunnerUnit { observed: o } => HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["the cgroup leaf '", o as String, "' is not an actions-runner@ unit: this job is not running inside a runner slot on the subject host"], "") }
    RunnerUnitInstanceMalformed { observed: o, cause: c } => HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["the cgroup leaf '", o as String, "' names an actions-runner@ instance that cannot be parsed: ", c as String], "") }
    RunnerUnitInstance { registration_name: i } => HolderIdentified { unit: runner_unit_name_of_registration(registration_name: i) }
  }
}

// THE FULL IDENTIFICATION. Each refusal names the leg that failed, because an operator reading a withheld
// converge has to be able to tell "this job was not dispatched from a slot" apart from "the slot's
// registration moved underneath the job" -- the first is a dispatch-shape bug and the second is a race.
// EVERY failure leg answers HolderUnidentified, and the driver withholds on it: identification is what
// makes the holder's exclusion safe, so a job that cannot say who it is converges nobody.
fn bracket_holder_identify(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, units: List<NonEmptyStr>) -> BracketHolder
{
  let self_read = Filesystem.Read(path: procfs_path_literal(p: ProcSelfCgroup) as String)
  if self_read.success {
    match read_unified_cgroup_membership(content: self_read.content) {
      CurrentCgroupObserved { relative_path: rel } =>
        match bracket_holder_unit_of_leaf(leaf: bracket_holder_leaf(relative_path: rel as String)) {
          HolderUnidentified { cause: c } => HolderUnidentified { cause: c }
          HolderIdentified { unit: unit } =>
            // THE UNIT MUST BE ONE THIS PASS MANAGES. If the job runs on a slot the desired shape does not
            // list (a slot mid-decommission, or a dispatch landed on a leftover runner), excluding it from
            // the bracket is meaningless -- withhold instead of converging a host around a slot nobody owns.
            if length(filter(units, u => u == unit)) == 0 {
              HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["the job's own unit '", unit as String, "' is not a desired slot of this host: the converge job is running somewhere this pass does not manage"], "") }
            } else {
              match actions_variable_read(name: github_runner_name_variable_name) {
                ActionsVariableAbsent { variable: v, detail: d } => HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["the job carries no ", v, " (", d, "): a converge job dispatched outside a runner job has no verifiable holder"], "") }
                ActionsVariablePresent { value: env_name } =>
                  match slot_current_registration_over_unit(target: target, context: context, unit: unit) {
                    UnitRegistrationUnreadable { unit: u, cause: c } => HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["the current registration of the job's own unit '", u as String, "' is unreadable (", c, "): the environment cannot be joined to the host"], "") }
                    UnitRegistrationRead { unit: u, instance: _, name: n } =>
                      if (n as String) != env_name {
                        HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["the job's ", github_runner_name_variable_name, " is '", env_name, "' but the current registration of its own unit '", u as String, "' is '", n as String, "': the environment and the host disagree about who this job is"], "") }
                      } else {
                        HolderIdentified { unit: u }
                      }
                  }
              }
            }
        }
      // EVERY non-observed arm answers who-is-this with its own located cause (review 86323, finding 2:
      // no fabricated "unreachable" diagnostic -- these arms match the FAILURE cases by name, and the
      // observed case is the happy path above, so the type system keeps the impossible case out).
      MembershipMissing => HolderUnidentified { cause: "the job's own cgroup has no unified (v2) membership line: this is not a cgroupv2 host, and caps have no meaning here" }
      MembershipAmbiguous { first: f, second: s } => HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["the job's own cgroup reports two unified memberships ('", f as String, "' and '", s as String, "'): the holder could be either"], "") }
      MembershipMalformed { cause: ProcSelfCgroupLineMissingColon } => HolderUnidentified { cause: "the job's own /proc/self/cgroup carries a line with no hierarchy separator" }
      MembershipMalformed { cause: ProcSelfCgroupLineMissingSecondColon } => HolderUnidentified { cause: "the job's own /proc/self/cgroup carries a line with no path separator" }
      MembershipMalformed { cause: ProcSelfCgroupLineEmptyHierarchy } => HolderUnidentified { cause: "the job's own /proc/self/cgroup carries a line with an empty hierarchy field" }
      MembershipMalformed { cause: ProcSelfCgroupLineEmptyPath } => HolderUnidentified { cause: "the job's own /proc/self/cgroup carries a line with an empty path field" }
    }
  } else {
    HolderUnidentified { cause: join(["Filesystem.Read of ", procfs_path_literal(p: ProcSelfCgroup) as String, " failed: ", self_read.error, ": the job cannot say which unit it runs on"], "") }
  }
}

fn acquire_bracket_over(target: SshTarget, context: FleetSshExecutionContext, host: String, units: List<NonEmptyStr>) -> BracketAcquisition
{
  match owned_set_of(readings: map(units, u => read_slot_drain_over(target: target, context: context, unit: u))) {
    OwnedSetUnproven { units: us } => BracketNotAcquired { refusal: BracketRefusedObligationNotRecorded { cause: concat("a slot could not be read, so ownership is unproven: ", join(map(us, u => u as String), " ")) } }
    OwnedSet { units: owned } =>
      match write_bracket_obligation(host: host, units: owned, discharged: false) {
        Present { value: why } => BracketNotAcquired { refusal: BracketRefusedObligationNotRecorded { cause: why } }
        Absent => BracketAcquired { owned: owned }
      }
  }
}

// ── The holder deferral ledger ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// THE COUNT THAT BOUNDS THE HOLDER'S DEFERRAL (review 86146, finding 2; DESIGN §4b meta-obligation 2: a
// class below ceiling names its next-rung trigger; §5: a degradation is countable, never quietly success).
// A DEFERRED-SELF holder is owed a bracket from a pass dispatched on ANOTHER slot; if placement keeps
// landing every pass on this same slot, the deferral must not be a permanent green. The count lives on
// the HOST, in the holder's own slot directory (see holder_deferral_ledger_path: a per-run file could
// not carry state across passes, and that is exactly the state this ledger exists to carry). It is
// keyed to the
// HOST AND THE UNIT: slot 02's debt is not slot 03's. A pass that finds the holder at desired DELETES the
// file (convergence clears the debt); a pass that finds it owed increments the count; past the fleet bound
// the cap module turns the same reading into a REFUSAL. A ledger this pass cannot read or write -- no
// no slot directory for the unit, an unreadable file -- yields an UNCOUNTED deferral, typed as such: uncounted is greppable
// and red-flagged on the wire, never silently treated as counted or unbounded.
type HolderDeferralLedgerFile {
  key: String
  deferred_passes: Int
}

data holder_deferral_ledger_file_name: String = "runner-host-cap-holder-deferral"

fn holder_deferral_ledger_key(host: String, unit: String) -> String {
  join(["host=", host, " unit=", unit], "")
}

// THE LEDGER LIVES IN THE HOLDER'S OWN SLOT DIRECTORY (/opt/actions-runner/<instance>/), NOT RUNNER_TEMP.
// RUNNER_TEMP is per-run and cleaned between runs, so a count kept there resets on every pass and the
// bound below could never fire -- the very hole the bound exists to close. The slot directory is durable,
// per-unit, and is the same tree the pass already reads (.runner_path) and the same account owns. An
// instance the unit does not name has no directory and no ledger: that is unwritable, not fresh.
fn holder_deferral_ledger_path(unit: NonEmptyStr) -> String?
{
  match read_runner_unit_name(observed: unit) {
    RunnerUnitInstance { registration_name: i } => Present { value: join([actions_runner_base_dir, "/", i as String, "/", holder_deferral_ledger_file_name], "") }
    RunnerUnitInstanceMalformed { observed: _, cause: _ } => none
    NotARunnerUnit { observed: _ } => none
  }
}

fn holder_deferral_ledger_text(o: HolderDeferralLedgerFile) -> String {
  serialize_json(v: json_object(members: [json_kv(key: "key", value: json_string(s: o.key)), json_kv(key: "deferred_passes", value: json_int(n: o.deferred_passes))]))
}

type HolderDeferralLedgerReading
  = LedgerReadingAbsent
  | LedgerReadingHeld { passes: Int }
  | LedgerReadingStale { found: String }
  | LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: String }

fn holder_deferral_ledger_reading_of_text(text: String, expected_key: String) -> HolderDeferralLedgerReading {
  match parse_json_document(s: text) {
    JsonDocumentUnreadable { gap: g } => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: concat("the deferral ledger is not a JSON document: ", json_document_gap_text(gap: g)) }
    JsonDocumentParsed { value: doc } =>
      match doc {
        JsonObject { members: ms } =>
          if length(ms) != 2 {
            LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: "the deferral ledger does not carry exactly the members key and deferred_passes" }
          } else {
            match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: "key") {
              JsonMemberFound { value: JsonString { value: k } } =>
                if k != expected_key {
                  LedgerReadingStale { found: k }
                } else {
                  match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: "deferred_passes") {
                    JsonMemberFound { value: JsonNumber { lexeme: l } } =>
                      match parse_int(s: l) {
                        Present { value: n } => if n < 0 { LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: "the deferral ledger counts a negative number of passes" } } else { LedgerReadingHeld { passes: n } }
                        Absent => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: concat("the deferral ledger's deferred_passes is not an integer: ", l) }
                      }
                    _ => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: "the deferral ledger's deferred_passes member is missing, duplicated or not a number" }
                  }
                }
              _ => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: "the deferral ledger's key member is missing, duplicated or not a string" }
            }
          }
        _ => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: "the deferral ledger is not a JSON object" }
      }
  }
}

fn holder_deferral_ledger_reading_of(observation: FilesystemFileObservation, expected_key: String) -> HolderDeferralLedgerReading {
  match observation {
    FilesystemFileAbsent(_) => LedgerReadingAbsent
    FilesystemFileRead { path: _, content: c } => holder_deferral_ledger_reading_of_text(text: c, expected_key: expected_key)
    FilesystemFileIndeterminate { cause: c } => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: c }
    FilesystemFileObservationsDisagree { path: _, cause: c } => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: c }
    FilesystemFileSubjectRefused { directory: _, name: _, cause: c } => LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: c }
  }
}

// THE LEDGER READING, FOLDED TO THE CAP MODULE'S INPUT. Stale and unreadable both become UNCOUNTED (with
// the cause named), never Fresh: a file that exists but cannot be trusted says the host HAS deferral
// state this pass cannot see, and pretending freshness would restart the bound for free.
fn holder_deferral_ledger_read(host: String, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> HolderDeferralLedger
{
  let key = holder_deferral_ledger_key(host: host, unit: unit as String)
  match holder_deferral_ledger_path(unit: unit) {
    Absent => LedgerUnwritable { cause: "the holder unit names no parseable slot instance, so it has no slot directory to carry a deferral ledger" }
    Present { value: path } =>
      match holder_deferral_ledger_reading_of(observation: filesystem_file_observation_of_path(path: path), expected_key: key) {
        LedgerReadingAbsent => LedgerFresh
        LedgerReadingHeld { passes: n } => LedgerCount { prior: n }
        LedgerReadingStale { found: k } => LedgerUnwritable { cause: concat("the ledger on this host belongs to another subject: ", k) }
        LedgerReadingUnreadable { cause: c } => LedgerUnwritable { cause: c }
      }
  }
}

// RECORDING ONE MORE DEFERRED PASS, OR CLEARING THE DEBT. Both return a cause instead of raising: a
// ledger that cannot be written makes the NEXT pass's reading uncounted, which the wire already names;
// it must never itself be the reason a cap phase withholds.
fn holder_deferral_ledger_record(host: String, unit: NonEmptyStr, passes: Int) -> String?
{
  match holder_deferral_ledger_path(unit: unit) {
    Absent => Present { value: "the holder unit names no parseable slot instance, so the ledger has nowhere to live" }
    Present { value: path } => {
      let w = Filesystem.Write(path: path, content: holder_deferral_ledger_text(o: HolderDeferralLedgerFile { key: holder_deferral_ledger_key(host: host, unit: unit as String), deferred_passes: passes }))
      if w.success { none } else { Present { value: w.error } }
    }
  }
}

fn holder_deferral_ledger_clear(host: String, unit: NonEmptyStr) -> String?
{
  match holder_deferral_ledger_path(unit: unit) {
    Absent => none
    Present { value: path } => {
      // An effect result, read like every other delete in the repo (workspace_commissioning_
      // apply_readback.dag:181): success covers both deleted and already-absent, and a refusal
      // carries its error -- the typed variant arms are the kernel's, not route vocabulary.
      let removed = Filesystem.Delete(path: path)
      if removed.success { none } else { Present { value: join(["delete of ", path, " refused: ", removed.error], "") } }
    }
  }
}

// THE LEDGER STEP THE DRIVER RUNS AROUND THE HOLDER'S VERDICT. One place decides what the host's count
// does for each verdict shape: converged clears the debt; deferred counts one more pass (from whatever
// the read said, fresh = 1); ANY refusal -- unread cgroup, or the bound having blown -- leaves the count
// exactly as it was: a refusal is not a pass where progress was possible, and a blown bound must stay red
// until something actually changes. Returns a cause when the bookkeeping itself could not land; the
// driver reports it on the wire but never lets it change the verdict.
fn holder_ledger_after_verdict(host: String, unit: NonEmptyStr, verdict: SlotCapOutcome, ledger: HolderDeferralLedger) -> String?
{
  match verdict {
    SlotCapsSelfAtDesired { unit: _ } => holder_deferral_ledger_clear(host: host, unit: unit)
    SlotCapsSelfDeferred { unit: _, detail: _ } =>
      // A deferral means the fold READ a ledger (an unreadable one refuses, see
      // slot_cap_holder_deferral_outcome); the Unwritable arm here is defensive recovery: if a pass
      // ever defers on an unreadable count, restart the count at 1 -- never leave it uncounted.
      match ledger {
        LedgerCount { prior: p } => holder_deferral_ledger_record(host: host, unit: unit, passes: p + 1)
        _ => holder_deferral_ledger_record(host: host, unit: unit, passes: 1)
      }
    // THE RESET AFTER THE LOUD RED STEP (review 86323): the ledger refused the run by name; this pass
    // is the counted, loud step that earns the overwrite. Rewrite it at pass 1 so the NEXT pass reads a
    // real count and can defers normally, with the bound live again. A stale-key or corrupted file costs
    // one red run -- not a permanent green, and not a silent widen either: the overwrite only happens
    // behind a refusal the operator sees on the wire.
    SlotCapsRefused { unit: _, step: s, reason: _ } =>
      if s == "holder-deferral-ledger" {
        match ledger {
          LedgerUnwritable { cause: _ } => holder_deferral_ledger_record(host: host, unit: unit, passes: 1)
          _ => none
        }
      } else {
        none
      }
    _ => none
  }
}
